decisions:
  - id: TD-2026-04-28-atlas-scope
    decision: Atlas is advisory-only (no write permissions except arch.yaml itself)
    rationale: Architectural drift detection is high-value read-only analysis; write authority limited to the arch manifest prevents Atlas from accidentally mutating code or .purpose files during audit passes
  - id: TD-2026-04-28-arch-yaml-feature-tier
    decision: arch feature-tier activates only when .paradigm/arch.yaml exists
    rationale: Zero-cost for projects that haven't adopted arch.yaml; no tool noise until user opts in by creating the manifest
learnings:
  - arch.yaml maps tiers (front-end, back-end, aux services) with tech stacks and component membership — fills the gap between paradigm symbols (what exists) and architecture understanding (how layers relate)
  - cartographer archetype fires after Builder in orchestration so architectural changes are detected in the same session they're made
  - Feature-tier detect gate pattern (fs.existsSync) is the cleanest way to add opt-in MCP tools without polluting the tool list on every project
